        "Watch verified requests by operator, path and purpose alongside identifiable assistant referrals. A useful ratio is verified HTML fetches divided by referred human landings for the same operator and period. Label it clearly: it is not a conversion rate and cannot prove that a given fetch caused a given click. Cloudflare uses a similar [crawl-to-refer measure](https://blog.cloudflare.com/ai-search-crawl-refer-ratio-on-radar/) at network scale and notes that it can swing sharply when referrals are sparse.",
        "For a young website, one click more or less can make a ratio jump dramatically. Publish raw counts with the ratio, use weekly or monthly windows and avoid ranking platforms by a metric based on a handful of visits. If a bot requests your documentation repeatedly but never sends clicks, first ask whether those documents answer questions completely and whether visitors would have a reason to follow a link."
